\newlabel{eqn:demix}{{4}{3}{Spinodal stability from demixing free-energy curvature}{equation.3.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces (a) Demixing free energy $\Delta G_{\mathrm  {demix}} = -\Delta G_{\mathrm  {mix}}$ (Eq.\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {eqn:demix}) versus chromium composition $x_{\mathrm  {Cr}}$ at 298.15 K: muffin-tin (EMTO-CPA, ferromagnetic) enthalpies of Korzhavyi \textit  {et al.} \cite  {korzhavyi2009electronic}, present GGA calculations with VASP (filled) and Quantum ESPRESSO (40-atom SQS, references FM Fe and AFM Cr; open circles at the PBE-relaxed lattice parameter, open diamonds at the room-temperature experimental lattice parameter with the references at theirs, which changes $\Delta G_{\mathrm  {demix}}$ by at most 10 meV/atom), calorimetric enthalpies of Dench \cite  {dench1963adiabatic} (measured near 1600 K relative to paramagnetic Fe and Cr), the CALPHAD bcc description of Andersson and Sundman \cite  {andersson1987thermodynamic} (non-magnetic part, line), and the PWSCF--PAW and SIESTA--NC interaction models of Levesque \textit  {et al.} \cite  {levesque2011simple}. All series include the ideal configurational entropy as in Eq.\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {eqn:demix}; the measured vibrational entropy of mixing, about $0.2\,k_{\mathrm  {B}}$/atom \cite  {fultz1995phonon}, would change $\Delta G_{\mathrm  {demix}}$ by at most 5 meV/atom at this temperature. (b) Curvature of the PWSCF--PAW demixing free energy. Solid line: exact analytic second derivative of the model. Filled circles: three-point second differences of its tabulated values on the non-uniform composition grid. Open circles: two-point second differences of the same values, with a quadratic least-squares fit (dashed). Shading marks positive demixing curvature, i.e., negative mixing curvature, which identifies spinodally unstable compositions. (c) Fully decomposed ($\phi =1$) MD structures, viewed as whole $25^3$ \textit  {bcc} cells (three outer faces, corner sublattice; Sec.\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {sec:md}) at the indicated Cr contents; red, Fe; blue, Cr. Inside the spinodal window the alloy separates into interpenetrating Fe-rich $\alpha $ and Cr-rich $\alpha '$ domains, whereas at the dilute ends the minority element forms only isolated clusters. (d) The DFT cells: \textit  {bcc} unit cells of Fe and Cr and 40-atom SQS ($2\times 2\times 5$ conventional cells), relaxed in Quantum ESPRESSO, with their local magnetic moments (arrows; red, Fe; blue, Cr). The Cr moments, antiparallel to Fe at 20\% Cr, vanish between 35 and 50\% Cr.\relax }}{4}{figure.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:free_energy}{{1}{4}{(a) Demixing free energy $\Delta G_{\mathrm {demix}} = -\Delta G_{\mathrm {mix}}$ (Eq.~\ref {eqn:demix}) versus chromium composition $x_{\mathrm {Cr}}$ at 298.15 K: muffin-tin (EMTO-CPA, ferromagnetic) enthalpies of Korzhavyi \textit {et al.} \cite {korzhavyi2009electronic}, present GGA calculations with VASP (filled) and Quantum ESPRESSO (40-atom SQS, references FM Fe and AFM Cr; open circles at the PBE-relaxed lattice parameter, open diamonds at the room-temperature experimental lattice parameter with the references at theirs, which changes $\Delta G_{\mathrm {demix}}$ by at most 10 meV/atom), calorimetric enthalpies of Dench \cite {dench1963adiabatic} (measured near 1600 K relative to paramagnetic Fe and Cr), the CALPHAD bcc description of Andersson and Sundman \cite {andersson1987thermodynamic} (non-magnetic part, line), and the PWSCF--PAW and SIESTA--NC interaction models of Levesque \textit {et al.} \cite {levesque2011simple}. All series include the ideal configurational entropy as in Eq.~\ref {eqn:demix}; the measured vibrational entropy of mixing, about $0.2\,k_{\mathrm {B}}$/atom \cite {fultz1995phonon}, would change $\Delta G_{\mathrm {demix}}$ by at most 5 meV/atom at this temperature. (b) Curvature of the PWSCF--PAW demixing free energy. Solid line: exact analytic second derivative of the model. Filled circles: three-point second differences of its tabulated values on the non-uniform composition grid. Open circles: two-point second differences of the same values, with a quadratic least-squares fit (dashed). Shading marks positive demixing curvature, i.e., negative mixing curvature, which identifies spinodally unstable compositions. (c) Fully decomposed ($\phi =1$) MD structures, viewed as whole $25^3$ \textit {bcc} cells (three outer faces, corner sublattice; Sec.~\ref {sec:md}) at the indicated Cr contents; red, Fe; blue, Cr. Inside the spinodal window the alloy separates into interpenetrating Fe-rich $\alpha $ and Cr-rich $\alpha '$ domains, whereas at the dilute ends the minority element forms only isolated clusters. (d) The DFT cells: \textit {bcc} unit cells of Fe and Cr and 40-atom SQS ($2\times 2\times 5$ conventional cells), relaxed in Quantum ESPRESSO, with their local magnetic moments (arrows; red, Fe; blue, Cr). The Cr moments, antiparallel to Fe at 20\% Cr, vanish between 35 and 50\% Cr.\relax }{figure.caption.1}{}}
