\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces (a) Rayleigh SAW frequency $f_{\mathrm  {SAW}}$ versus chromium composition $x_{\mathrm  {Cr}}$ ($\lambda =6.4\,\mu $m) for the literature (black), present DFT (VASP, filled red; Quantum ESPRESSO at the PBE-relaxed lattice parameter, open circles, and at the experimental lattice parameter, filled blue diamonds), and MD random-alloy (green) data of Fig.\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:elastic_elastodynamics}. At each MD composition, small dots ($\phi =0.6$--0.9) and an open square ($\phi =1$) show the frequency of the decomposing structure, each series anchored to the random-alloy value at that composition, so the ladder height is the stiffening on decomposition (as in b). Below the data, the star marks Fe--36Cr \cite  {rae2026tgs} and the arrows indicate schematically the partitioning toward Fe-rich $\alpha $ and Cr-rich $\alpha '$ during spinodal decomposition; dotted lines rise from each composition toward the data. Arrow endpoints are illustrative, not measured phase compositions. (b) $f_{\mathrm  {SAW}}$ versus spinodal conversion fraction $\phi $ from the MD (EAM) decomposition simulations (Sec.\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {sec:md}): all twelve compositions, $x_{\mathrm  {Cr}}=0.05$--0.95, from the present $25^3$-cell calculations (squares, coloured by $x_{\mathrm  {Cr}}$; Fe$_{50}$Cr$_{50}$ in this cell is the check of the original series), and equiatomic Fe$_{50}$Cr$_{50}$ from Ref.\nobreakspace  {}\cite  {al2023detecting} (black circles, $50^3$ cell, fixed density 7535 kg m$^{-3}$). $\phi =0.5$ is the random alloy and $\phi =1$ the fully separated structure. (c) The whole $25^3$ MD cell of Fe$_{64}$Cr$_{36}$ (three outer faces) from $\phi =0.5$ to 1.0 (red, Fe; blue, Cr): Fe-rich $\alpha $ and Cr-rich $\alpha '$ domains form and coarsen as $\phi $ increases; these are the structures whose frequencies are plotted in (b). All $25^3$-cell values are means over five MD alloys (random seeds); their $\pm 1$ s.d. error bars (at most 0.5 MHz) are smaller than the symbols.\relax }}{7}{figure.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:fsaw_combined}{{3}{7}{(a) Rayleigh SAW frequency $f_{\mathrm {SAW}}$ versus chromium composition $x_{\mathrm {Cr}}$ ($\lambda =6.4\,\mu $m) for the literature (black), present DFT (VASP, filled red; Quantum ESPRESSO at the PBE-relaxed lattice parameter, open circles, and at the experimental lattice parameter, filled blue diamonds), and MD random-alloy (green) data of Fig.~\ref {fig:elastic_elastodynamics}. At each MD composition, small dots ($\phi =0.6$--0.9) and an open square ($\phi =1$) show the frequency of the decomposing structure, each series anchored to the random-alloy value at that composition, so the ladder height is the stiffening on decomposition (as in b). Below the data, the star marks Fe--36Cr \cite {rae2026tgs} and the arrows indicate schematically the partitioning toward Fe-rich $\alpha $ and Cr-rich $\alpha '$ during spinodal decomposition; dotted lines rise from each composition toward the data. Arrow endpoints are illustrative, not measured phase compositions. (b) $f_{\mathrm {SAW}}$ versus spinodal conversion fraction $\phi $ from the MD (EAM) decomposition simulations (Sec.~\ref {sec:md}): all twelve compositions, $x_{\mathrm {Cr}}=0.05$--0.95, from the present $25^3$-cell calculations (squares, coloured by $x_{\mathrm {Cr}}$; Fe$_{50}$Cr$_{50}$ in this cell is the check of the original series), and equiatomic Fe$_{50}$Cr$_{50}$ from Ref.~\cite {al2023detecting} (black circles, $50^3$ cell, fixed density 7535 kg m$^{-3}$). $\phi =0.5$ is the random alloy and $\phi =1$ the fully separated structure. (c) The whole $25^3$ MD cell of Fe$_{64}$Cr$_{36}$ (three outer faces) from $\phi =0.5$ to 1.0 (red, Fe; blue, Cr): Fe-rich $\alpha $ and Cr-rich $\alpha '$ domains form and coarsen as $\phi $ increases; these are the structures whose frequencies are plotted in (b). All $25^3$-cell values are means over five MD alloys (random seeds); their $\pm 1$ s.d. error bars (at most 0.5 MHz) are smaller than the symbols.\relax }{figure.caption.3}{}}
