\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S40}{\ignorespaces Where in composition the surface waves of Fe--Cr reach the band that pristine CASS cannot produce ($>609$\nobreakspace  {}MHz at $\lambda =6.4\,\mu $m), and what decomposition of Fe--36Cr does to it. Split violins show the distribution of the highest (P)SAW frequency of each of $10^4$ randomly oriented grains at each composition (tick: maximum). (a) QE 40-atom SQS, random by construction: experimental lattice parameter (left halves) and PBE-relaxed lattice parameter (right halves). (b) MD $25^3$ cell, tensors averaged over five MD alloys: random, $\phi =0.5$ (left halves) and fully decomposed, $\phi =1$ (right halves) at the same composition. (c) Fraction of grains in the band. Decomposition moves the material of Fe--36Cr to the $\alpha $ and $\alpha '$ compositions (0.14 and 0.88, CALPHAD at 748\nobreakspace  {}K \cite  {xiong2011improved}); the lever rule then gives the fraction for decomposed Fe--36Cr (star in c). With the QE tensors at the experimental lattice parameter, the reference set for elastic moduli (Section S8), random alloys stay below the band up to 80\% Cr and the band is reached only on the Cr-rich side (5\% of grains at 95\% Cr, 13\% for AF Cr); decomposed Fe--36Cr has about 0.8\% of grains in the band, all from $\alpha '$, against none as a random alloy. The 80\% Cr tensor, with antiparallel Cr moments and a soft $C_{12}$, stays below the band, so the $\alpha '$ value interpolated at 0.88 (2.7\%) is uncertain. The PBE-relaxed QE tensors reach the band at most compositions (up to 22\%), a consequence of their too-stiff bulk moduli; the classical MD tensors stay below it at every composition, random or decomposed. Scripts: \texttt  {scripts/run\_saw\_landscape.py}, \texttt  {scripts/make\_saw\_landscape.py}.\relax }}{46}{figure.caption.62}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:S_saw_landscape}{{S40}{46}{Where in composition the surface waves of Fe--Cr reach the band that pristine CASS cannot produce ($>609$~MHz at $\lambda =6.4\,\mu $m), and what decomposition of Fe--36Cr does to it. Split violins show the distribution of the highest (P)SAW frequency of each of $10^4$ randomly oriented grains at each composition (tick: maximum). (a) QE 40-atom SQS, random by construction: experimental lattice parameter (left halves) and PBE-relaxed lattice parameter (right halves). (b) MD $25^3$ cell, tensors averaged over five MD alloys: random, $\phi =0.5$ (left halves) and fully decomposed, $\phi =1$ (right halves) at the same composition. (c) Fraction of grains in the band. Decomposition moves the material of Fe--36Cr to the $\alpha $ and $\alpha '$ compositions (0.14 and 0.88, CALPHAD at 748~K \cite {xiong2011improved}); the lever rule then gives the fraction for decomposed Fe--36Cr (star in c). With the QE tensors at the experimental lattice parameter, the reference set for elastic moduli (Section S8), random alloys stay below the band up to 80\% Cr and the band is reached only on the Cr-rich side (5\% of grains at 95\% Cr, 13\% for AF Cr); decomposed Fe--36Cr has about 0.8\% of grains in the band, all from $\alpha '$, against none as a random alloy. The 80\% Cr tensor, with antiparallel Cr moments and a soft $C_{12}$, stays below the band, so the $\alpha '$ value interpolated at 0.88 (2.7\%) is uncertain. The PBE-relaxed QE tensors reach the band at most compositions (up to 22\%), a consequence of their too-stiff bulk moduli; the classical MD tensors stay below it at every composition, random or decomposed. Scripts: \texttt {scripts/run\_saw\_landscape.py}, \texttt {scripts/make\_saw\_landscape.py}.\relax }{figure.caption.62}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S41}{\ignorespaces Spin- and charge-density wave of \textit  {bcc} Cr computed in a $1\times 1\times 20$ supercell of the conventional cell (40 atoms along [001], room-temperature experimental $a=2.8846$ \r A, $k=16\times 16\times 1$, other settings as the SQS runs). The supercell sets the incommensurability $\delta =1/20$, close to the measured 0.048 \cite  {fawcett1988spin}. (a) Site moments of the SDW, which has nodes every $\lambda _{\mathrm  {SDW}}/2=29$ \r A, with the commensurate AFM moments (dots) for comparison. (b) Site charges relative to their mean. They oscillate with period $\lambda _{\mathrm  {SDW}}/2$, the charge-density wave that accompanies the SDW, with a peak-to-peak amplitude of about 0.01 e. (c) Forces along $Q$ at the unrelaxed positions, also with period $\lambda _{\mathrm  {SDW}}/2$; they drive the strain wave seen by X-ray diffraction \cite  {fawcett1988spin}. The static continuum response to their $2\mathbf  {Q}$ harmonic, $u=F_{2Q}/(C_{11}\Omega q^2)$, is $u\approx 0.02$ \r A\ ($\varepsilon =qu\approx 0.5\%$), an upper bound given the PBE moments. (d) From the electrons to the SAW. Left: the magnetic pressure of the AF and SDW cells relative to the non-magnetic one (5.8 and 4.2 GPa) is proportional to $\langle m^2\rangle $ (ratios 0.73 and 0.71), the Landau magnetovolume coupling. Right: in pure Cr (QE, 2-atom cells) AF order lowers $K$ by 29\% but leaves $G$ unchanged ($+0.6\%$), so $f_{\mathrm  {SAW}}$ falls by only 1.0\%. Cr magnetism therefore acts on the volume channel, moves the SAW by about 1\%, and its strain wave, with a period of 29 \r A, averages out over the 6.4 $\mu $m TGS wavelength; the $+5\%$ stiffening on decomposition is a shear effect (Fig.\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:S_embrittlement}). In PBE the SDW is 12.7 meV/atom below the non-magnetic state but 7.3 meV/atom above the commensurate AFM, and its moments (up to 1.16 $\mu _{\mathrm  B}$) exceed the measured 0.62 $\mu _{\mathrm  B}$. Both are known shortcomings of semilocal DFT for Cr \cite  {hafner2001does,cottenier2002what}, so the figure shows the form of the coupled spin, charge and strain waves, not their measured amplitudes. Script: \texttt  {dft\_qe/sdw\_cr.py}.\relax }}{47}{figure.caption.63}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:S_sdw}{{S41}{47}{Spin- and charge-density wave of \textit {bcc} Cr computed in a $1\times 1\times 20$ supercell of the conventional cell (40 atoms along [001], room-temperature experimental $a=2.8846$ \AA , $k=16\times 16\times 1$, other settings as the SQS runs). The supercell sets the incommensurability $\delta =1/20$, close to the measured 0.048 \cite {fawcett1988spin}. (a) Site moments of the SDW, which has nodes every $\lambda _{\mathrm {SDW}}/2=29$ \AA , with the commensurate AFM moments (dots) for comparison. (b) Site charges relative to their mean. They oscillate with period $\lambda _{\mathrm {SDW}}/2$, the charge-density wave that accompanies the SDW, with a peak-to-peak amplitude of about 0.01 e. (c) Forces along $Q$ at the unrelaxed positions, also with period $\lambda _{\mathrm {SDW}}/2$; they drive the strain wave seen by X-ray diffraction \cite {fawcett1988spin}. The static continuum response to their $2\mathbf {Q}$ harmonic, $u=F_{2Q}/(C_{11}\Omega q^2)$, is $u\approx 0.02$ \AA \ ($\varepsilon =qu\approx 0.5\%$), an upper bound given the PBE moments. (d) From the electrons to the SAW. Left: the magnetic pressure of the AF and SDW cells relative to the non-magnetic one (5.8 and 4.2 GPa) is proportional to $\langle m^2\rangle $ (ratios 0.73 and 0.71), the Landau magnetovolume coupling. Right: in pure Cr (QE, 2-atom cells) AF order lowers $K$ by 29\% but leaves $G$ unchanged ($+0.6\%$), so $f_{\mathrm {SAW}}$ falls by only 1.0\%. Cr magnetism therefore acts on the volume channel, moves the SAW by about 1\%, and its strain wave, with a period of 29 \AA , averages out over the 6.4 $\mu $m TGS wavelength; the $+5\%$ stiffening on decomposition is a shear effect (Fig.~\ref {fig:S_embrittlement}). In PBE the SDW is 12.7 meV/atom below the non-magnetic state but 7.3 meV/atom above the commensurate AFM, and its moments (up to 1.16 $\mu _{\mathrm B}$) exceed the measured 0.62 $\mu _{\mathrm B}$. Both are known shortcomings of semilocal DFT for Cr \cite {hafner2001does,cottenier2002what}, so the figure shows the form of the coupled spin, charge and strain waves, not their measured amplitudes. Script: \texttt {dft\_qe/sdw\_cr.py}.\relax }{figure.caption.63}{}}
