\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S43}{\ignorespaces Spinodal wavelength of the decomposing MD structures. As in the image analysis of Ref.\nobreakspace  {}\cite  {al2023detecting}, each structure is turned into a black-and-white Cr map: the $25^3$ cell is coarse-grained onto its conventional cubic cells, smoothed over one cell and thresholded at the alloy composition (Cr-rich or Fe-rich). (a) Two-point correlation $C(r)$ of the map of Fe--36Cr from the random alloy ($\phi =0.5$) to full decomposition ($\phi =1$): the correlated Cr-rich regions grow from about 1 to 2 nm. (b) Wavelength from the chord lengths, $\lambda =\langle l_{\rm  Cr}\rangle +\langle l_{\rm  Fe}\rangle $ (mean length of the Cr-rich and Fe-rich runs along all lattice lines, i.e.\ one Fe/Cr period), against $\phi $ (mean $\pm $ s.d. of five alloys), with the 2D-FFT values of Ref.\nobreakspace  {}\cite  {al2023detecting} for Fe$_{50}$Cr$_{50}$ ($50^3$ cells, open circles) and its STEM-EDX value for CF8 aged at 400\nobreakspace  {}$^\circ $C for 10 kh (dashed, 4.76 nm). (c) $\lambda $ against Cr content at every $\phi $ (colours as in a). The random alloy gives 2.6--2.7 nm at every composition, the scale of the one-cell smoothing and so the floor of the measure. Inside the spinodal window the wavelength grows from there to 3.3 nm at $\phi =0.6$ and 4.2--4.4 nm at $\phi =1$, the trend and scale of Ref.\nobreakspace  {}\cite  {al2023detecting} (4.2--4.8 nm) and of the measured 4.76 nm. Outside the window the minority element forms isolated clusters, and the long matrix runs make $\lambda $ larger without a spinodal period. A 3D FFT of the same maps gives a structure factor that keeps rising towards the lowest $k$ the 7.2\nobreakspace  {}nm box allows, so its wavelength is set by the cell size; the chord lengths are not quantised in this way. The conversion procedure (Sec.\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {sec:md}) is a Monte Carlo construction, not aging kinetics, so $\phi $ is not a time. Script: \texttt  {scripts/spinodal\_wavelength.py}.\relax }}{50}{figure.caption.66}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:S_spinodal_wavelength}{{S43}{50}{Spinodal wavelength of the decomposing MD structures. As in the image analysis of Ref.~\cite {al2023detecting}, each structure is turned into a black-and-white Cr map: the $25^3$ cell is coarse-grained onto its conventional cubic cells, smoothed over one cell and thresholded at the alloy composition (Cr-rich or Fe-rich). (a) Two-point correlation $C(r)$ of the map of Fe--36Cr from the random alloy ($\phi =0.5$) to full decomposition ($\phi =1$): the correlated Cr-rich regions grow from about 1 to 2 nm. (b) Wavelength from the chord lengths, $\lambda =\langle l_{\rm Cr}\rangle +\langle l_{\rm Fe}\rangle $ (mean length of the Cr-rich and Fe-rich runs along all lattice lines, i.e.\ one Fe/Cr period), against $\phi $ (mean $\pm $ s.d. of five alloys), with the 2D-FFT values of Ref.~\cite {al2023detecting} for Fe$_{50}$Cr$_{50}$ ($50^3$ cells, open circles) and its STEM-EDX value for CF8 aged at 400~$^\circ $C for 10 kh (dashed, 4.76 nm). (c) $\lambda $ against Cr content at every $\phi $ (colours as in a). The random alloy gives 2.6--2.7 nm at every composition, the scale of the one-cell smoothing and so the floor of the measure. Inside the spinodal window the wavelength grows from there to 3.3 nm at $\phi =0.6$ and 4.2--4.4 nm at $\phi =1$, the trend and scale of Ref.~\cite {al2023detecting} (4.2--4.8 nm) and of the measured 4.76 nm. Outside the window the minority element forms isolated clusters, and the long matrix runs make $\lambda $ larger without a spinodal period. A 3D FFT of the same maps gives a structure factor that keeps rising towards the lowest $k$ the 7.2~nm box allows, so its wavelength is set by the cell size; the chord lengths are not quantised in this way. The conversion procedure (Sec.~\ref {sec:md}) is a Monte Carlo construction, not aging kinetics, so $\phi $ is not a time. Script: \texttt {scripts/spinodal\_wavelength.py}.\relax }{figure.caption.66}{}}
